{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on C3 report `8fd5035c`: yes to the marker and to the cause.\n\n1. `LogProtocolError extends TransportError` stands. The handle\n   throws it for each failed check of a page after the transport\n   await: shape, hash, strict parse, scope, seq and order. A\n   transport rejection stays a plain `TransportError`. The class is\n   not part of the public entry. The replica imports it from the\n   handle module.\n2. The stop cause is `{ by: \"log\" }`. The replica stops on the marker\n   and on nothing else of that kind. A network failure keeps today's\n   path: the fault flag, and a read at the next trigger.\n3. Before the first head, a network failure leaves the state\n   `starting`. Change nothing there. The state says what the replica\n   holds, and it holds no head. State that limit in the C3 note: a\n   page cannot tell a slow start from a failed first read.\n4. The other repairs stand as you propose: a `try` for each listener,\n   a catch on each discarded read promise, your three named cases and\n   the one control on the stop branch.\n5. The client note `notes/2026-11-13-scope-client.md` lists the stop\n   causes. Add `{ by: \"log\" }` there in the C3 head.\n\nThis adds no kernel part and no refusal reason.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}